<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Just-in-Time software defect prediction (JIT-SDP) plays a critical role in prioritizing
risky code changes during code review and continuous integration. However,
existing datasets often suffer from noisy labels and low precision in
identifying bug-inducing commits. To address this, we present ReDef (<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re</span>vert-based <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Def</span>ect dataset), a
high-confidence benchmark of function-level modifications curated from 22
large-scale C/C++ projects. Defective cases are anchored by revert commits,
while clean cases are validated through post-hoc history checks. Ambiguous
instances are conservatively filtered out via a GPT-assisted triage process
involving multiple votes and audits. This pipeline yields 3,164 defective and 10,268 clean modifications,
offering substantially more reliable labels than prior existing resources.
Beyond dataset construction, we provide the first systematic evaluation of how
pre-trained language models (PLMs) reason about code
modifications—specifically, which input encodings most effectively expose
change information, and whether models genuinely capture edit semantics. We
fine-tune CodeBERT, CodeT5+, and UniXcoder under five encoding strategies,
and further probe their sensitivity through counterfactual perturbations that
swap added/deleted blocks, invert diff polarity, or inject spurious markers. Our
results show that compact diff-style encodings consistently outperform whole-function
formats across all PLMs, with statistical tests confirming large,
model-independent effects. However, under counterfactual tests, performance
degrades little or not at all—revealing that what appears to be robustness in
fact reflects reliance on superficial cues rather than true semantic
understanding. These findings indicate that, unlike in snapshot-based tasks,
current PLMs remain limited in their ability to genuinely comprehend code
modifications.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Just-in-Time Software Defect Prediction, Pre-trained Language Models, Code Change Analysis, Software Defect Prediction
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Software defect prediction (SDP) aims to estimate whether a given piece of code is likely to contain defects so that limited engineering resources—testing, review time, and Continuous Integration (CI) capacity—can be prioritized where they matter most <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malhotra, 2015</a>)</cite>. Unlike defect detection or localization, which seeks to identify concrete bug instances, SDP is a risk estimation problem used to triage changes and guide quality assurance activities. Effective SDP reduces downstream failures and lowers maintenance costs by surfacing risky code early enough for developers to take action within their normal workflow—for example, during code review or before a merge in CI.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Classical SDP typically models static snapshots of code or aggregates hand-crafted process metrics—such as churn, complexity, ownership, and past defects—at the file, module, or function level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wahono, 2015</a>)</cite>. While such predictors can correlate with risk, they fail to capture how the code is evolving at the moment of change. Just-in-Time (JIT) SDP addresses this limitation by reasoning over code modifications at commit time, aligning closely with practical review and CI processes that scrutinize the changed lines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamei et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2008</a>)</cite>. However, most existing JIT-SDP datasets rely on SZZ(Śliwerski–Zimmermann–Zeller)-style linkages from bug-fixing commits back to bug-introducing commits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Śliwerski et al., 2005</a>)</cite>. SZZ attributes the introduction of a defect to the last modifier of the lines changed by a later fix—an assumption that is often violated by routine edits such as renaming or refactoring. This approach introduces substantial noise: it can misidentify the true introduction points, propagate commit-level label errors, and mismatch the actual defective lines, ultimately obscuring evaluation quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herbold et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. To mitigate the noise inherent in SZZ-based labeling, we turn to a more explicit and high-confidence signal: revert commits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Reverts are a routine part of disciplined software development. Developers may revert commits for various reasons—such as regressions, incompatibilities, performance degradations, policy or formatting violations, or simply because another change has superseded the patch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. A revert creates an explicit, time-stamped link to the earlier change. When the revert message cites a failure or fix, it provides strong evidence that the prior modification was defective. We therefore use revert commits as anchors to harvest high-confidence positive examples, and subsequently filter out cases unrelated to defective modifications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With a more reliable labeling strategy in place, the next question is how modern models can be effectively leveraged. Existing JIT-SDP research has largely depended on compact engineered features with classical machine learning (ML) and, more recently, deep learning (DL) models. However, these approaches primarily capture surface-level correlations—such as churn, ownership, and developer activity—rather than the true semantics of code changes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamei et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamei et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. In other words, they estimate probabilities from shallow cues instead of reasoning about what the code actually does.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, recent code-focused pre-trained language models (PLMs) and large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong performance across diverse SE tasks—such as code search, summarization, repair, and vulnerability detection—precisely because they are designed to capture the meaning of code itself, not merely its statistical correlates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This raises a natural question for JIT-SDP: if PLMs can effectively understand static code snapshots, can they also reason about code modifications, i.e., the relationship between the before and after states? Given that PLMs have consistently shown strong effectiveness for code understanding tasks, we therefore focus our study on PLMs as the primary models for systematic evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To answer this question, we first construct a high-confidence, change-centric benchmark. Next, in Stage 1, we compare alternative ways of presenting modification information to PLMs, asking which input encodings yield the most effective performance.
For this, we fine-tune three widely used code PLMs—CodeBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, CodeT5+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, and UniXcoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2022</a>)</cite>—as backbones.
However, strong results under these encodings alone do not guarantee genuine semantic reasoning, as models may still rely on superficial cues. Therefore, in Stage 2, we deliberately perturb the representation of code changes—e.g., by swapping added and deleted code blocks, inverting edit polarity, or injecting spurious markers—to test whether PLMs degrade when true semantic information is distorted.
By probing how models behave under such distortions, our protocol not only measures raw accuracy but also uncovers a blind spot in current PLM research: apparent robustness may simply reflect reliance on shallow cues rather than genuine semantic reasoning about code changes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High-Confidence JIT-SDP Dataset (ReDef):</span> We curate ReDef (<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re</span>vert-based <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Def</span>ect dataset), a corpus of single-function changes harvested via revert anchors and refined through GPT-assisted filtering. The dataset is further validated through manual sampling to ensure benchmark reliability, and its 10K+ (3,164 defective, 10,268 clean) scale makes it well-suited for fine-tuning PLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Encoding Strategies for PLMs:</span> We conduct the first systematic comparative study of five encoding strategies for representing code modifications as PLM inputs, providing clear empirical evidence of how input representations affect change-centric defect prediction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Counterfactual Robustness Analysis:</span> We design counterfactual perturbation tests to probe whether PLMs truly reason about code changes beyond surface cues, revealing limitations of current models and offering guidance for future research.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these contributions provide both a reliable benchmark and empirical insights into how PLMs handle code changes, informing future defect prediction research and practice.
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews background and related work. Section 3 describes our dataset construction and PLM input strategies. Section 4 outlines the experimental setup, and Section 5 reports results. Section 6 discusses threats to validity, and Section 7 concludes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background and Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reviews prior research on software defect prediction from three perspectives. Section 2.1 outlines classical <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Software Defect Prediction (SDP)</em>, which predicts defect-prone modules from static snapshots using either hand-crafted metrics or learned representations. Section 2.2 covers <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Just-in-Time software defect prediction (JIT-SDP)</em>, which shifts the unit of analysis to commits or patches and incorporates both engineered features and recent neural approaches. It also summarizes the limitations of existing JIT-SDP datasets, such as noise introduced by SZZ-based labeling. Finally, Section 2.3 discusses advances in applying <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pre-trained Language Models (PLMs)</em> across software engineering (SE) tasks, motivating their use for change-level defect prediction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Software Defect Prediction (SDP)</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical SDP focuses on artifact-level prediction from static snapshots, typically at the file, class, or module granularity. Early approaches rely on hand-crafted product metrics (e.g., size, complexity, coupling) and process/organization metrics (e.g., churn, revisions, developer ownership, past defects) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malhotra, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bell et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huda et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. These features are commonly fed into traditional learners such as logistic regression, random forests, and Support Vector Machines (SVMs). For example, Elish et al. showed that SVMs perform competitively on NASA datasets compared to logistic regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elish and Elish, 2008</a>)</cite>, while ensemble methods based on random forests also address class imbalance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balaram and Vasundra, 2022</a>)</cite>. Decision tree–based models have similarly achieved strong performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khoshgoftaar and Seliya, 2002</a>)</cite>.
More recent work replaces hand-crafted metrics with learned representations, leveraging topic models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, deep generative models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, or transformer-based encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Omri and Sinz, 2020</a>)</cite>. Evaluation often considers effort-aware measures (e.g., recall at top-x% LOC) and cross-project transferability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bell et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ostrand and Weyuker, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tosun et al., 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turhan et al., 2009</a>)</cite>. Despite this progress, snapshot-based prediction cannot capture the semantics of specific changes, and file- or module-level labels blur defective regions—complicating causal interpretation and limiting actionable insights for change-level prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Just-in-Time Software Defect Prediction (JIT-SDP) and Datasets</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">JIT-SDP shifts the focus from static artifacts to change-time risk estimation, scoring each commit or patch so that reviewers and CI gates can provide immediate feedback. The concept originates from Kim et al., who first classified individual changes as clean or buggy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2008</a>)</cite>. Subsequent work engineered compact commit-level features—such as churn, diffusion, prior defect density, developer experience, recency, and message cues—and applied machine learning techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamei et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shihab et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamei et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. Later studies incorporated commit messages and patch text, while neural models began encoding diffs directly. For example, Yang et al. proposed Deeper, a Deep Belief Network (DBN)-based model that outperformed logistic regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, and Hoang et al. introduced DeepJIT, which integrates commit messages and code metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. CC2Vec further embedded code changes hierarchically to capture structural and sequential information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, researchers have applied PLMs to JIT-SDP.
Guo et al. systematically compared six transformer-based backbones (RoBERTa, CodeBERT, BART, PLBART, GPT2, and CodeGPT) and showed that PLM-based variants consistently outperform traditional baselines such as DeepJIT and CC2Vec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Building on this, Abu Talib et al. demonstrated that parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) techniques such as LoRA can further boost JIT-SDP performance on ApacheJIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abu Talib et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while Lin et al. introduced CCT5—a CodeT5-initialized model pre-trained on CodeChangeNet—that achieves state-of-the-art results on code-change tasks including JIT-SDP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Jiang et al. extended CodeBERT with a Replaced Message Identification objective, resulting in BiCC-BERT and JIT-BiCC, which improved F1-scores by over 10% <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Together, these studies highlight the promise of PLMs for JIT-SDP, yet it remains unclear whether current gains reflect genuine modeling of code-change semantics or incidental signals from existing datasets—underscoring the need for both more reliable benchmarks and systematic evaluation of model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advances, a critical limitation of existing JIT-SDP datasets lies in label reliability.
Afric et al. reported that even with RoBERTa-based issue classification, file-level datasets contained on average 14.3% mislabeled instances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Afric et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Herbold et al. showed that SZZ-based labeling is particularly error-prone, with only about half of the commits identified as bug-inducing being correct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herbold et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Several variants of SZZ attempt to mitigate this noise—for example, by excluding refactoring edits that standard SZZ mistakenly flags as defect-inducing or by tracing commit chains more accurately to recover missed bug-introducing links <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, even with such refinements, existing datasets still fall short in reliability, underscoring the need for explicit validation of defect labels, which we address in RQ1.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Pre-trained Language Models (PLMs) in Other Software Engineering Tasks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pre-trained language models (PLMs) trained on large code corpora—such as CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT, UniXCoder, and CodeT5—have been increasingly applied to diverse SE tasks, often achieving state-of-the-art results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In code review, Li et al. proposed CodeReviewer, a Transformer-based encoder–decoder PLM tailored for diff review, improving both comment generation and code refinement quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Similarly, Kartal et al. demonstrated that transformer-based similarity measures outperform prior baselines in automated comment recommendation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kartal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In program repair, PLMs have achieved significant progress. For instance, Mashhadi and Hemmati applied CodeBERT to repair Java bugs, achieving 19–72% fix accuracy on ManySStuBs4J datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mashhadi and Hemmati, 2021</a>)</cite>. Retrieval-augmented methods like RAP-Gen combine CodeT5 with external bug-fix retrieval, showing marked improvements over previous approaches—repairing 15 more bugs on the Defects4J benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>. Additionally, AlphaRepair uses CodeBERT in a zero-shot, infilling-style repair setup and achieves state-of-the-art results without task-specific retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In vulnerability detection, PLMs have shown strong effectiveness across diverse settings. For example, one study fused multi-modal knowledge (structural, semantic, and syntactic features) with PLM encoders to achieve state-of-the-art function-level vulnerability detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Atiiq et al. fine-tuned PLMs on the multi-language CVEFixes dataset (JavaScript, Java, Python, PHP, Go, C/C++), reporting substantial variation across languages and finding that UniXcoder outperformed other models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Atiiq et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. More recently, a comprehensive benchmark demonstrated that PLMs can surpass much larger code LLMs in accuracy and F1-scores, underscoring their superior inductive bias and efficiency for vulnerability detection tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, PLMs have shown impressive capabilities across diverse SE tasks. Importantly, many of these tasks—such as program repair and vulnerability detection—require reasoning about the semantics of code edits or risky patterns, which parallels the demands of defect prediction. Their strong performance in such semantics-sensitive settings suggests that PLMs are particularly promising for change-aware defect prediction, motivating our systematic investigation in this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our methodology comprises two main pillars. (A) ReDef Construction: We curate defective and clean code modifications
from large-scale C/C++ projects, leveraging revert signals, post-hoc history screening, and expert audits to ensure high label reliability.
(B) PLM Evaluation Protocol: We design a two-stage framework to assess how models
interpret code modifications. Figure 1 summarizes the overall approach.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09192v1/figure1.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/301;" width="476" height="301" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Overall approach of ReDef construction and PLM evaluation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>ReDef Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We curated ReDef, a high-confidence JIT-SDP corpus from 22 long-lived, high-activity C/C++ repositories. Projects were selected for sustained commit volume, explicit revert workflows, and domain diversity to reduce project-specific bias. The curation yielded 3,164 defective and 10,268 clean modifications. Figure 2 reports per-project counts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09192v1/figure2.PNG" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/247;" width="381" height="247" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Number of defective and clean modifications per project in the ReDef corpus.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defective Modification.</span>
We extracted commits that touched exactly one file and whose modified lines were confined to a single function.
This design ensures reliable causal attribution to a specific function—a choice also adopted in recent datasets such as PrimeVul <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
From this single-function pool, we identified defective modifications by collecting revert commits whose messages
(i) contained the keyword “revert” and (ii) explicitly referenced the hash of an earlier commit.
Since such reverts typically indicate that the referenced commit introduced a problem,
the targeted commits are highly likely to represent defective modification.
For each referenced hash, we retrieved its associated commit (the target of the revert)
and collected four artifacts for analysis: the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reverted commit message</span>,
the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_before</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_after</span> from that commit, and the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">revert commit message</span> itself.
Together these formed an evidence bundle and were submitted to GPT-4o three independent times under a fixed rubric to remove ambiguous or non-defect reverts (e.g., formatting changes, policy reversions, or hash-only messages without rationale). The rubric distinguished each revert as: (A) Failed/incomplete fix (Defective<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Defective), (B) Bug introduction (Clean<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Defective), or (C) Other (non-defect). Categories (A) and (B) were merged into the defective class, while (C) served as a filter to discard non-defect cases.
To ensure high precision, a candidate was retained only if <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all three GPT-4o votes unanimously fell in <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">{</span>A,B<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">}</span></em> (e.g., A/A/A or A/B/B). Any sample with one or more (C) votes (e.g., A/A/C) was conservatively discarded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clean Modification.</span>
To assemble the clean stratum, we first identified all defective modifications and then targeted clean modification commits from a similar time period in the same repository,
thereby mitigating both project-specific and period-specific bias.
As with the defective side, we extracted commits that touched exactly one file and whose modified lines were confined to a single function.
For each candidate commit, we inspected up to five subsequent commits that touched the same function.
If any of these later messages contained problematic keywords (e.g., “revert,” “rollback,” “fix bug,” “regression”),
the candidate was discarded to avoid false-clean labels—since such signals in subsequent edits suggest that the earlier commit may have introduced issues—an idea conceptually similar to SZZ methodology.
Surviving candidates were further required to be modified at least once after their introduction, which excluded dead or unused code.
We then formed an evidence bundle—<span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">commit message</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_before</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_after</span>—
and queried GPT-4o three independent times under a dedicated clean-label rubric. The rubric classified each commit as: (A) Bug fix (Defective<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Clean), (B) Improvement/optimization/feature change (Clean<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Clean), or (C) Ambiguous/irrelevant commits. Categories (A) and (B) were retained as clean modifications, while any case with a (C) vote was filtered out. The sample was kept only when all three votes unanimously fell in {A,B} (e.g., A/A/A or A/B/B).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table 1 summarizes the filtering outcomes. The defective pool was reduced from 6,257 to 3,164—about half of revert-anchored commits were discarded, mostly because the reasons for the reverts were not sufficiently specified. Among the retained cases, 1,270 were classified as Defective<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Defective and 1,795 as Clean<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Defective. By contrast, the clean pool was only modestly reduced (11,807 to 10,268), with 6,524 Defective<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Clean and 3,744 Clean<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Clean cases retained, all meeting the unanimous voting criterion. The exact GPT prompts and rubric are available in the provided artifact.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Filtering outcomes for defective (revert-anchored) and clean (history-screened) candidates.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:172.5pt;height:43.1pt;vertical-align:-20.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-85.1pt,21.3pt) scale(0.503355014408783,0.503355014408783) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Category</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Before GPT Filtering</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After GPT Filtering</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Defective total</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">6,257</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3,164</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">   Defective<math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Defective</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">–</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">1,270</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">   Clean<math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Defective</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">–</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">1,795</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Clean total</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">11,807</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10,268</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">   Defective<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Clean</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">–</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">6,524</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">   Clean<math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Clean</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">–</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">3,744</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Clean selection was intentionally conservative: a function had to be revised at least once after the candidate commit, and no problematic keywords could appear in the next five commits touching that function. Unlike prior JIT corpora (e.g., SZZ-derived <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamei et al., 2016</a>)</cite>) or static snapshot datasets (e.g., Devign <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, Big-Vul <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, Defects4J <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Just et al., 2014</a>)</cite>) that define “clean” at a snapshot or immediate post-fix state, our per-function, post-hoc history check aimed to reduce false-clean labels arising from late-emerging faults or follow-up fixes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">After assembling the labels, each repository was split time-respectively (80%/10%/10% into train/validation/test by commit timestamp) to avoid temporal leakage—training on commits occurring after the test set would otherwise expose future information.
Class balance (defective/clean) was preserved within each split, and splitting was performed per repository to ensure that every project was represented across partitions, preventing cases where a project might be entirely absent from validation or test.The per-project partitions were then concatenated to form the final splits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To characterize input length, we tokenized <span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_after</span> using each of the PLMs employed in our experiments (CodeBERT, UniXcoder, CodeT5+).
As shown in Figure 3, the cumulative distribution indicates that although the exact counts vary slightly by model, around 55–65% of functions exceed the 512-token limit, meaning the
entire function cannot be fed to the model without truncation. With UniXcoder’s 1,024-token limit, roughly 70% of
functions fit in full. This distribution motivates a comparison between encoding strategies that provide only the edited lines versus those that supplying the full function at the risk of truncation.
Although the dataset is derived from commit history, the intended inference unit is a function-level modification, which can be evaluated pre-commit (e.g., in an Integrated Development Environment (IDE) or local CI hooks) as well as at review/merge time.
To assess label reliability, two authors independently audited a stratified sample of 100 defective commits under a shared rubric and adjudicated disagreements.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09192v1/figure3.PNG" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/251;" width="381" height="251" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Cumulative distribution of function lengths (tokens) across PLM tokenizers</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>PLM Evaluation Protocol</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to assess whether pre-trained language models (PLMs) can effectively leverage
code-change information for defect prediction. To this end, we designed a two-stage evaluation.
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1</span> compares alternative input encodings that vary in how much context and change signal
are provided. <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2</span> introduces counterfactual perturbations that deliberately distort change signals, checking whether models degrade as expected when edit semantics are disrupted.
Together, these stages provide a structured protocol for evaluating whether PLMs rely on genuine semantic cues rather than superficial patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1</span>, we compared five alternative input encodings, each tokenized using the model’s native tokenizer before being fed into the PLM encoder.</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After-only</span>: The function_after, without any explicit indication of where edits occurred. Serves as a classical SDP-style baseline.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After+Change markers</span>: The function_after, augmented with <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CHG&gt;</span> tags on each modified line so the model can directly identify edited regions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Before+After</span>: Both pre- and post-modification functions concatenated, prefixed with markers (“[Before]: …[After]: …”). To balance length, half of the token budget was allocated to each side, ensuring that the after-code was not entirely truncated by a long before-code.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff with tags</span>: Only the changed lines and their local context, with <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;ADD&gt;</span> and <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;DEL&gt;</span> tags prepended to additions and deletions respectively, following the representation of DeepJIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Added<math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Deleted blocks</span>: Inspired by JiT-Fine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, we supplied added lines first, then deleted lines, each under explicit headers (“[ADDED LINES]: …[DELETED LINES]: …”). Unlike JiT-Fine, commit messages were excluded for fairness.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The first three variants serve as heuristic baselines, representing straightforward ways of exposing code modifications to PLMs without relying on prior proposals.
These five encodings let us quantify the trade-off between context and change signal under realistic token budgets and identify the most effective representation for defect prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2</span>,
we applied controlled perturbations to test whether models behave consistently when change signals are deliberately altered.
Each perturbation targeted the representation of change information while leaving the overall code form largely intact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia and Liang, 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spurious change markers</span>: In the <span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After+Change markers</span> format, the number of modified lines is first computed, then the same number of <span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CHG&gt;</span> tags are randomly inserted at arbitrary locations, injecting meaningless signals during both training and testing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Swapped snapshots</span>: Under the <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Before+After</span> format, models are trained identically, but at test time the positions of <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_before</span> and <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_after</span> are swapped with 50% probability (e.g., “[Before]: function_after [After]: function_before”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reversed diff tags</span>: Under the <span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff with tags</span> format, models are trained identically, but tested with the <span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;ADD&gt;</span> and <span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;DEL&gt;</span> tags inverted.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Swapped added/deleted blocks</span>: In the <span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Added<math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Deleted blocks</span> format, the two sections are randomly swapped with 50% probability, so that “[ADDED LINES]” contains deletions and “[DELETED LINES]” contains additions during both training and testing.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">These counterfactual variants are designed to reduce model performance: if a PLM truly understands the relationship between code before and after—that is, the semantics of the modification—then disrupting this information should lead to a drop in performance compared to the corresponding Stage 1 results.
Conversely, if performance remains largely unchanged, it suggests that the model has not learned meaningful change semantics and is instead relying on superficial cues from the overall code context.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Research Questions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We try to answer following research questions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How accurate are the labels in the ReDef JIT-SDP dataset?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Which input encodings yield the best PLM performance?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Does PLM performance degrade under counterfactual perturbations of change representations?</em></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Models and Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate how PLMs capture the semantics of code changes, we experimented with the following pre-trained models. CodeT5+ and CodeBERT were used with their default maximum input limit of 512 tokens.
UniXcoder uniquely supports longer contexts, so we evaluated it under both 512- and 1,024-token limit settings to test performance under constrained and full-capacity conditions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pre-trained Language Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CodeBERT.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2020</a>)</cite>
We included CodeBERT, a 125M parameter RoBERTa-based encoder pre-trained for code understanding. Despite being one of the earlier PLMs, it has consistently shown strong and robust performance in defect prediction and classification tasks, making it an essential baseline for fair comparison <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dahou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CodeT5+.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>
We used the 220M encoder-only variant, as detection tasks do not require generation capabilities. CodeT5+ has achieved state-of-the-art results across a wide range of software engineering tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, making it a competitive and modern baseline.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UniXcoder.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2022</a>)</cite>
We used the base-nine version (220M parameters, released in 2023), which has demonstrated strong performance across diverse SE tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to these PLMs, we implemented input encodings inspired by <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepJIT</span> (diff with <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;ADD&gt;</span>/<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;DEL&gt;</span> tags) and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">JIT-Fine</span> (added<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>deleted block format). These were not used as standalone baselines, but rather as representation strategies integrated into our Stage 1 evaluation protocol.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate model performance using five standard classification metrics: Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1 (F1-score), and PR-AUC.
Among these, F1 is adopted as the primary metric, as it balances precision and recall and is widely used for imbalanced datasets such as JIT-SDP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoang et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamei et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Accuracy}=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Accuracy</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Accuracy}=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Accuracy measures the overall proportion of correctly classified modifications, but can be misleading under severe class imbalance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Precision}=\frac{TP}{TP+FP},\quad\text{Recall}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Precision</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>Recall</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Precision}=\frac{TP}{TP+FP},\quad\text{Recall}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Precision quantifies the fraction of predicted defective modifications that are truly defective, while Recall measures the fraction of actual defective modifications that are successfully identified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{F1}=\frac{2\cdot\text{Precision}\cdot\text{Recall}}{\text{Precision}+\text{Recall}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>F1</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Precision</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Recall</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Precision</mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>Recall</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{F1}=\frac{2\cdot\text{Precision}\cdot\text{Recall}}{\text{Precision}+\text{Recall}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, and is widely regarded as the most informative single measure in imbalanced classification settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PR-AUC}=\int_{0}^{1}\text{Precision}(r)\,dr" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PR-AUC</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Precision</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PR-AUC}=\int_{0}^{1}\text{Precision}(r)\,dr</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">PR-AUC is the area under the Precision–Recall curve, which provides a threshold-independent view of the trade-off between precision and recall across all operating points.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each PLM–encoding configuration, we fine-tuned the model ten times with different random seeds (12345–12354) and report the mean and standard deviation to reduce stochastic variance. All models were trained under consistent hyperparameters: a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\mathrm{e}{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\mathrm{e}{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size 8, and the AdamW optimizer with decoupled weight decay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017</a>)</cite>. Gradient accumulation was applied to stabilize updates, and a linear learning-rate scheduler with a 10% warm-up ratio was used to avoid abrupt changes in the early stages. Each run lasted for 6 epochs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The best checkpoint was selected based on validation F1 (our primary metric) and subsequently used for test reporting. To address class imbalance, we applied the standard weighting scheme, assigning the positive class a weight of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{neg}}/N_{\text{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>neg</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>pos</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{neg}}/N_{\text{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">King and Zeng, 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments were conducted on Tesla V100-PCIE-32GB GPUs in a shared academic cluster. Due to CPU bottlenecks and scheduling constraints, full GPU capacity was not always utilized. Training time was consistent across encoding strategies since only the input format varied; for reference, a single CodeBERT run at the 512-token limit required approximately 30 minutes on one GPU.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical Analysis.</span>
For RQ2, we conducted a two-way repeated-measures ANOVA with within-subject factors <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model</span> (4) and <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">encoding</span> (5), treating the 10 seeds as subjects. We report partial <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as effect sizes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lakens, 2013</a>)</cite>. Post-hoc comparisons used paired <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests with Holm step-down correction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holm, 1979</a>)</cite> applied per family: Encoding main-effect pairs on subject-level marginal means (collapsed over models), and all encoding pairs within each model (simple effects).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For RQ3, each perturbation was paired with its corresponding original encoding and evaluated across the same 10 seeds. We tested the directional hypothesis that perturbations degrade performance using one-sided paired <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests (<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}:F1_{\text{orig}}&gt;F1_{\text{pert}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mtext>pert</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}:F1_{\text{orig}}&gt;F1_{\text{pert}}</annotation></semantics></math>), complemented by Wilcoxon signed-rank tests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilcoxon, 1945</a>)</cite>. We also report paired effect size as Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and non-parametric percentile 95% bootstrap confidence intervals (CIs) for the mean difference <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=F1_{\text{orig}}-F1_{\text{pert}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mtext>pert</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=F1_{\text{orig}}-F1_{\text{pert}}</annotation></semantics></math> using 10,000 resamples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tibshirani and Efron, 1993</a>)</cite>. <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values were adjusted for multiple comparisons using Holm’s method within each set of tests (e.g., across the five perturbations).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Following common conventions, we consider results statistically significant at <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. Effect sizes are interpreted using standard thresholds: partial <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}\approx 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}\approx 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> (small), <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.06</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.06</annotation></semantics></math> (medium), <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.14</annotation></semantics></math> (large); Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}\approx 0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}\approx 0.2</annotation></semantics></math> (small), <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> (medium), <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math> (large) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen, 2013</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experimental results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> <em id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How accurate are the labels in the ReDef JIT-SDP dataset?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We manually audited 100 randomly sampled defective functions. Following recent practices in vulnerability dataset curation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, two authors independently inspected the commit evidence—<span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reverted commit message</span>, <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_before</span>, <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_after</span>, and <span id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">revert commit message</span>—and labeled each case as <em id="S5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Correct</em>, <em id="S5.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ambiguous</em>, or <em id="S5.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Wrong</em>. After adjudication, 92 cases were classified as Correct, 5 as Ambiguous, and 3 as Wrong, yielding a positive-label precision of 0.92 as shown in Table 2. Compared to prior reports of 14–50% noise in SDP datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herbold et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Afric et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, our 8% mislabel rate is substantially lower.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Outcomes of the manual audit for 100 randomly sampled defective labels.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:172.5pt;height:36.5pt;vertical-align:-16.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-30.2pt,6.4pt) scale(0.740473016229418,0.740473016229418) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correct</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ambiguous</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wrong</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Reviewer 1</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">94</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">1</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Reviewer 2</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">83</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">14</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">3</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Adjudicated</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">92</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">3</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Ambiguities initially accounted for  30% of cases, typically arising when revert messages
cited only a hash without any rationale, forcing annotators to infer defect-relatedness. We revised
the rubric to abstain unless defect links were explicit, which reduced ambiguous cases to 5%
in the final adjudicated set. The three Wrong cases followed non-defect patterns (e.g., mistaken commit ordering, temporary mitigations, or structural refactoring).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results suggest that our revert-anchored, history-screened pipeline yields comparatively reliable defect labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="112.58" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 112.58" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,112.58) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCCC;" fill="#CCCCCC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 106.68 C 0 109.94 2.64 112.58 5.91 112.58 L 471.47 112.58 C 474.73 112.58 477.38 109.94 477.38 106.68 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 88.47 L 475.41 88.47 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 97.07)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S5.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Summary of RQ1</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:4.35em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="62.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 60.19)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.p5.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S5.p5.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A manual audit of 100 defective functions found that 92% were correctly labeled, with only 8% identified as ambiguous or wrong. This demonstrates our revert-anchored, history-screened pipeline provides substantially more reliable labels than prior SDP datasets.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> <em id="S5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Which input encodings yield the best PLM performance?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Table 3 summarizes the performance of all PLM–encoding combinations. Overall,
<span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff w/ tags</span> and <span id="S5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Added<math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Deleted</span> consistently achieved the highest F1 across models,
whereas <span id="S5.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After+Markers</span> and <span id="S5.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Before+After</span> performed worst. In contrast, these latter two encodings often ranked highest in terms of accuracy.
This discrepancy is explained by class imbalance: ReDef contains over three times more clean (negative) than defective (positive) modifications,
so encodings biased toward the majority class can appear more accurate while sacrificing F1.
To compare models more rigorously beyond such raw patterns, we applied statistical tests as follows.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Stage 1 performance analysis of different encodings across models (mean ± std over 10 runs).</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:148.1pt;vertical-align:-72.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-123.9pt,53.2pt) scale(0.581992146862081,0.581992146862081) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Model</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Encoding</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">F1</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">PR-AUC</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Accuracy</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Precision</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Recall</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">CodeBERT</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">After-only</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3731\pm 0.0191" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3731</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0191</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3731\pm 0.0191</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3086\pm 0.0160" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3086</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0160</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3086\pm 0.0160</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5751\pm 0.0536" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5751</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0536</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5751\pm 0.0536</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2901\pm 0.0201" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2901</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0201</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2901\pm 0.0201</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5401\pm 0.0999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5401</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5401\pm 0.0999</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
