<td id="S5.T3.2.1.21.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T3.m102" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6050\pm 0.0930" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6050</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0930</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6050\pm 0.0930</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.21.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T3.m103" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2911\pm 0.0173" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2911</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0173</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2911\pm 0.0173</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.21.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.21.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline"><math id="S5.T3.m104" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4580\pm 0.2313" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4580</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.2313</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4580\pm 0.2313</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted a two-way repeated-measures ANOVA with <span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model</span> (4 PLMs) and <span id="S5.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">encoding</span> (5 variants) as within-subject factors (Table 4). Results showed significant main effects of both model (<math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(3,27)=9.96,p&lt;.001,\eta^{2}_{p}=0.53,f=1.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>9.96</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.53</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.05</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(3,27)=9.96,p&lt;.001,\eta^{2}_{p}=0.53,f=1.05</annotation></semantics></math>) and encoding (<math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(4,36)=13.80,p&lt;.001,\eta^{2}_{p}=0.61,f=1.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>36</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>13.80</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.61</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.24</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(4,36)=13.80,p&lt;.001,\eta^{2}_{p}=0.61,f=1.24</annotation></semantics></math>), both indicating large effect sizes. No interaction was found (<math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(12,108)=0.82,p=.63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12,108</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.82</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.63</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(12,108)=0.82,p=.63</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting that the relative ranking of encodings was consistent across PLMs.
Post-hoc pairwise tests with Holm correction revealed that <span id="S5.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff w/ tags</span> and <span id="S5.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Added<math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Deleted</span> significantly outperformed <span id="S5.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After+Markers</span> and <span id="S5.p8.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Before+After</span> (all <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{Holm}}&lt;.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Holm</mtext></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{Holm}}&lt;.01</annotation></semantics></math>), while performing comparably to each other. <span id="S5.p8.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After-only</span> showed intermediate performance, significantly better than some weaker encodings but worse than the top two in most cases. The pattern was consistent for CodeBERT, CodeT5+, and UniXcoder-512, whereas for UniXcoder-1,024 the differences diminished and were not statistically significant.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
