</figure>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Across models, this advantage of change-focused encodings can be explained by two factors. First, as shown in Figure 3, only about 30–40% of functions fit within 512 tokens, so encodings that pass the entire function (<span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After-only</span>, <span id="S5.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">After+Markers</span>, <span id="S5.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Before+After</span>) often truncate important edits. Second, supplying the entire function can also introduce excessive, mostly unchanged context that distracts the model from the actual modification. The weakness of <span id="S5.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Before+After</span> illustrates both issues: two full functions must fit under the same budget, increasing truncation risk and noise. Supporting the second explanation, UniXcoder performed worse at 1,024 tokens than at 512, suggesting that longer sequences add distracting context rather than improving focus.
Finally, in our setting which primarily targets code understanding, CodeBERT again emerged as the strongest PLM, in line with recent studies showing its robustness on classification and comprehension tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dahou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p10.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="228.04" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 228.04" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,228.04) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCCC;" fill="#CCCCCC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 222.14 C 0 225.4 2.64 228.04 5.91 228.04 L 471.47 228.04 C 474.73 228.04 477.38 225.4 477.38 222.14 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 203.93 L 475.41 203.93 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 212.53)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.p10.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S5.p10.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Summary of RQ2</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:12.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="178.35" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 175.66)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.p10.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S5.p10.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Diff-style encodings<span id="S5.p10.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span>Diff w/ tags<span id="S5.p10.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, </span>Added<math id="S5.p10.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Deleted<span id="S5.p10.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">) achieved the best performance by emphasizing changed lines and avoiding truncation or extraneous context.
In contrast, whole-function encodings lose critical signals, with </span>Before+After<span id="S5.p10.pic1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> being most affected.
</span>CodeBERT<span id="S5.p10.pic1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> showed the strongest performance overall, while UniXcoder suffered performance drops at longer token budgets due to added context noise.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> <em id="S5.p11.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Does PLM performance degrade under counterfactual perturbations of change representations?</em>
As shown in Table 5, across the four perturbations—<span id="S5.p11.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spurious change markers</span>, <span id="S5.p11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Swapped snapshots</span>, <span id="S5.p11.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reversed diff tags</span>, and <span id="S5.p11.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Swapped added/deleted blocks</span>—performance remained effectively unchanged.
Even when before/after alignment or polarity direction was disrupted, F1 differences stayed below 1%.
This stability is counterintuitive: if PLMs were truly leveraging semantic signals from code changes, such distortions should have produced clear drops in performance metrics.
Instead, the negligible differences suggest that the models are not semantically sensitive to modification content, but rather exploit surface-level regularities (e.g., token distributions, positional patterns) that persist under perturbation.
In other words, the robustness observed here reflects insensitivity rather than genuine understanding.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span>Stage 2 perturbation analysis with CodeBERT (mean ± std over 10 runs).</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
