</figure>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical tests (paired t-tests, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, bootstrap with Holm correction) confirmed this robustness.
Only <span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reversed diff tags</span> showed statistical significance (<math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0045" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0045</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0045</annotation></semantics></math> after correction), but the absolute effect size was negligible
(<math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0.0035" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0035</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0.0035</annotation></semantics></math>, Cohen’s <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}=1.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}=1.33</annotation></semantics></math>). In practice, such a tiny change does not indicate genuine semantic sensitivity.
Taken together, these results reveal an important limitation: current PLMs achieve stable
performance not because they robustly model code-change semantics, but because they fall back
on shallow cues that remain unaffected by perturbations. This highlights a fundamental gap between
snapshot-level code understanding and true modification reasoning, and points to the need for
edit-aware pre-training objectives or architectures explicitly designed to capture change semantics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p13.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="128.42" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 128.42" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,128.42) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCCC;" fill="#CCCCCC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 122.51 C 0 125.77 2.64 128.42 5.91 128.42 L 471.47 128.42 C 474.73 128.42 477.38 125.77 477.38 122.51 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 104.31 L 475.41 104.31 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 112.9)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.p13.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S5.p13.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Summary of RQ3</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:5.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="78.72" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.03)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.p13.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S5.p13.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PLM performance remained stable under all four perturbations, with F1 drops <math id="S5.p13.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
Even when polarity direction or before/after order was disrupted, statistical tests confirmed no practical degradation.
This robustness indicates reliance on surface-level cues rather than genuine code-change semantics.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Threats to Validity</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Internal Validity</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Choice of Input Encoding.</span> We evaluated five representative encodings, including formats inspired by prior JIT-SDP studies. However, given that all encodings ultimately derive from <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_before</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_after</span>, other ways of structuring or combining these views may provide richer signals of semantic change. For example, tree-structured or graph-based encodings could expose different aspects of program structure, and hybrid schemes may better capture interactions between edits and surrounding context. Exploring such alternatives remains an open avenue for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical Power.</span> All statistical tests were based on 10 independent runs with different seeds. While this design controls stochastic variance, a larger number of repetitions could yield stronger statistical power and narrower confidence intervals.
However, prior AI studies on code intelligence tasks rarely report extensive repetitions: many omit random seeds entirely or rely on a single run, and even the more rigorous works seldom exceed 10 repetitions due to the substantial computational cost of PLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grishina et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Running 30 or more trials per configuration is generally infeasible in practice. Our choice of 10 runs follows this common practice while still providing a reasonable trade-off between robustness and resource constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>External Validity</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Project Selection Bias.</span> Our dataset was drawn from large, long-lived infrastructure projects (e.g., Linux, FFmpeg, Chromium). These may differ from smaller applications or libraries in review culture, test maturity, and revert policies. We partially mitigated this risk by including diverse domains and reporting a leave-one-project-out analysis, but broader coverage is needed. In particular, industrial or proprietary projects could exhibit markedly different defect patterns, and including such settings would provide stronger external validity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-Language Applicability.</span> This study focused solely on C/C++ projects. Substantial syntactic and semantic differences across programming languages may limit the applicability of our findings. Extending evaluation to multi-language datasets is a key direction for future work. Such efforts should examine whether our observations hold consistently across different language families.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduced ReDef, a high-confidence JIT-SDP dataset of function-level modifications with both defective and clean cases: 3,164 defective and 10,268 clean function-level modifications, with manual audits confirming 92% label precision—substantially higher than the prior JIT-SDP corpora. Our curation pipeline combines revert anchoring, longitudinal history screening, and GPT-assisted triage with multiple votes and audits to reduce label noise. Unlike conventional SZZ-based resources which infer bug-inducing commits post hoc and often lack clean counterparts, ReDef offers the community a reliable benchmark for studying change-aware models, enabling reproducible comparison and facilitating exploration of edit-sensitive learning objectives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using ReDef, we fine-tuned three strong PLMs—CodeBERT, CodeT5+, and UniXcoder—under five input encodings. Compact, change-focused encodings and tagged diffs achieved the best results, whereas whole-function inputs often diluted the signal and suffered from truncation under token limits. Notably, UniXcoder performed worse at a 1,024-token budget than at 512, showing that more context does not necessarily help when change information becomes obscured.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Counterfactual perturbations of change representations yielded almost no performance degradation. Even when we swapped the order of deleted and added blocks or mislabeled unchanged lines as modified, F1 remained nearly identical. If PLMs had truly internalized the semantics of edits, such distortions should have impaired performance.
This outcome exposes a critical blind spot in how current PLMs are evaluated on code modifications: what appears to be robustness is, in fact, an artifact of surface-level cue reliance. Our findings caution against interpreting strong benchmark scores as evidence of genuine change understanding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Threats to validity remain. We examined only five encoding strategies, but alternative ways of presenting <span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_before</span> and <span id="S7.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">function_after</span> may provide richer semantic signals. Our dataset focuses on large C/C++ projects, leaving open questions about smaller repositories and other languages. Finally, although we repeated all experiments with ten random seeds, stronger statistical power could be obtained with more runs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Future work should probe why PLMs lag in modification understanding compared to single-snapshot tasks such as vulnerability detection. Promising directions include pre-training objectives that explicitly expose models to edits and diffs, as well as richer change representations or architectures that make modification semantics more accessible to PLMs. Beyond PLMs, evaluating decoder-based code LLMs (e.g., CodeLlama, StarCoder) and API models (e.g., GPT-4o) under the same protocol will test whether larger generative models can close this gap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We hope that ReDef and our evaluation protocol will serve as a foundation for the next generation of change-aware, semantics-sensitive defect prediction models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Data Availability</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An anonymous link containing the dataset, construction scripts, and experimental code is publicly available for reproducibility:<a href="https://figshare.com/s/4f202bc0921e26b41dc2" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://figshare.com/s/4f202bc0921e26b41dc2</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
